        <OtherAbstract Type="PIP" Language="eng">
            <AbstractText>This review examines whether the IUD is associated with increased risks in nulliparous as compared with parous women. Insertion in nulliparae must be gently done because of the narrower cervical canal, and may be aided by appropriate modifications of technique and anesthetic. Fewer explusions and removals are reported and risk of infection may be lessened with insertion between days 11-17. The most appropriate IUDs for nulliparae appear to be the Cu-7, Cu-T, and ML Cu250, because of their smaller size and consequent lower event rates for pregnancy, explusion, and medical removal. Operator experience and provision of clear verbal and written instructions are crucial. Nulliparae tend to have slightly higher event rates and therefore lower continuation rates than other women. Increased use of antiprostaglandins and improvement in size and shape of future IUDs should reduce incidence of spotting, bleeding, and cramping, major causes of discontinuation in nulliparous women. Recent work shows the relationship of PID to IUD use to be correlated with age and socioeconomic factors rather than parity. A past history of proven PID is a contraindication to IUD use. The IUD should be removed when a diagnosis of PID is made. Women at high risk of sexually transmitted disease should be discouraged from using IUDs. IUDs should not be the 1st choice of contraception in young women under 25 years.</AbstractText>
        </OtherAbstract>
